ected cycle and fingerprint and applies a greatest-event void order as the exclusive bound. An active-scope migration can be valid for maintenance yet remain globally non-current when a later valid foreign or prior-generation void bounds repository-wide validation; this is intentional, persists no foreign mutation, and requires completing or abandoning the active CHANGE before repository-wide repair. Void recording retains `No TDD cycle found for <testId>.` as an exit-2 error only when no cycle for the test exists in any scope. It then applies the active scope predicate when a CHANGE is active, otherwise considers all cycles for the test. Before verified-event targeting it retains the existing structural guards on the array-last cycle within that operation scope solely for backward-compatible missing-cycle-ID and missing-chain exit-2 errors; the missing-chain guard's companion “another cycle exists” test uses only cycles in the same operation scope. Those structural guards take precedence over valid-Green and already-voided message selection, and array position grants no target or fallback priority. It then computes the order-greatest verified event among terminal fingerprint evidence (Green, valid Refactor, or valid approved migration), valid voids, and work events for the candidate scope. A terminal-fingerprint greatest event belongs to a cycle with valid Green and preserves the existing valid-Green rejection regardless of whether Green, Refactor, or migration supplied that terminal order; a valid void greatest event preserves the existing already-voided rejection; and a greatest work event of either kind—valid Red on a cycle without valid Green, or linked non-passing Green—becomes the target when its cycle has no persisted void record and no valid Green. A cycle bearing an unverified void record is not eligible for re-targeting or overwrite and instead returns `{ voided: false, reason: "<testId> has no verifiable dangling TDD cycle in the current operation scope.